---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Projects, Tenants & Domains">
  <h1>Projects, Tenants &amp; Domains</h1>
  <p>
    Identity separates three concepts that older versions conflated into a
    single "tenant". Getting them straight is the key to the whole isolation
    model:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Project</strong> — the isolation boundary. Think of it as a
      Firebase project: one user pool, one set of credentials, one JWT issuer.
      Each Project maps onto exactly one storage scope (one shard).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tenant</strong> — a logical company or team entity
      <em>inside</em> a Project. Tenants form automatically from the email
      domains users sign up with (e.g. <code>acme.com</code>), or a signed-in
      user creates one where the Project allows
      <a href="#self-service-tenants">self-service tenants</a>. A single
      Project can hold many Tenants.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Domain</strong> — a DNS domain a Tenant registers and proves
      it controls. Verifying <code>acme.com</code> binds it to that one
      Tenant, so identity attributes <code>alice@acme.com</code> to it; until
      then the registration is only a claim, and several Tenants may hold
      one.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The Project is the isolation shard</h2>
  <p>
    The Project — not the tenant — is the unit of physical isolation. All
    per-request resolution starts by resolving the Project (the shard), either
    by API credential (<code>public_id</code> on a publishable / secret /
    mTLS credential) or by the <code>Host</code> header matching a configured
    project auth-domain. The user's Tenants are resolved <em>afterward</em>,
    from their memberships, and never select the Project. A token's
    <code>tenant</code> claim is the deployment default
    (<code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID</code>) unless the client
    <a href="#choosing-a-tenant-for-a-token">chooses a tenant</a> on refresh.
  </p>
  <p>
    This inversion means a single B2C product (millions of users, no company
    concept) and a B2B product (many company tenants) use the same model: one
    Project per product, Tenants grow underneath it without re-sharding.
  </p>

  <h2>The user pool is per Project; login is always by email</h2>
  <p>
    Users belong to a <strong>Project</strong>, not a Tenant. Uniqueness is
    <code>(project_id, lower(email))</code> — exactly one identity per person
    per Project, no matter how many company domains they touch. Authentication
    always resolves the identity by <code>(project_id, email)</code> first; the
    user's Tenants are derived afterward from their memberships, a domain one
    counting only while the email is verified and on a domain the tenant has
    verified. The Tenant is never an input to login, and signing in leaves the
    token's <code>tenant</code> claim at the deployment default.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`# A user proves: "I am alice@acme.com in project P"
# identity then derives: "...and acme.com makes her a member of Acme"
PasswordLogin(project=P, email="alice@acme.com", password=...)
  -> identity   (project_id=P, email=alice@acme.com)
  -> membership (Acme, from the verified acme.com domain; see ListMyTenants)
  -> token      ("tenant": GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID until a refresh chooses Acme)`}
    lang="bash"
  />

  <h2>Per-project serving domains</h2>
  <p>
    A Project can serve auth on one or more hostnames
    (<em>project auth-domains</em>). Incoming requests without an explicit
    credential are resolved to a Project by matching the <code>Host</code>
    header against <code>project_auth_domains.hostname</code>. One domain per
    project is marked primary (used to build links such as magic-link and
    OAuth callback URLs). The domain-management RPCs
    (<code>CreateDomain</code>, <code>VerifyDomain</code>,
    <code>AddProjectAuthDomain</code>, <code>SetPrimaryAuthDomain</code>) are
    documented in the API reference.
  </p>

  <h2>One Project per deployment, many on one datastore</h2>
  <p>
    A single Identity container serves one default Project, configured at
    startup. Multiple Projects can share one Postgres instance and stay fully
    isolated — every repository query is scoped by
    <code>WHERE project_id = $1</code>, which is the real isolation boundary and
    is backend-agnostic. On Postgres, row-level security adds defence-in-depth
    on top; the per-query project scope is what guarantees isolation on every
    backend.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`# Two products sharing one Postgres, isolated by Project
# Product A
docker run ... \\
  -e GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER=postgres \\
  -e GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DSN=postgres://.../identity \\
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID=proj_product_a \\
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:1.2.0

# Product B
docker run ... \\
  -e GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER=postgres \\
  -e GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DSN=postgres://.../identity \\
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID=proj_product_b \\
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:1.2.0`}
    lang="bash"
  />

  <h2 id="self-service-tenants">Self-service tenants</h2>
  <p>
    A Project can let its signed-in users create tenants of their own, for a
    team that has no company domain behind it. It is off by
    default; the Project turns it on in its <code>config_json</code> (the
    default Project takes it from
    <code>GATEWAY_SELF_SERVICE_TENANTS_ENABLED</code> when a request falls
    through to it; one that reaches the default Project by its own project
    key or auth hostname reads that Project's <code>config_json</code>
    instead):
  </p>
  <Code code={`{ "tenants": { "self_service": true } }`} lang="json" />
  <p>
    <code>CreateTenant(name)</code> then creates a claimed tenant and makes the
    caller its <code>owner</code>, in one transaction. Each user may create at
    most <code>GATEWAY_SELF_SERVICE_TENANTS_MAX_PER_USER</code> tenants (default
    10) per Project in <code>GATEWAY_SELF_SERVICE_TENANTS_WINDOW_SECONDS</code>
    (default one day); past that the call is refused with
    <code>RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED</code>. With the setting off it is
    <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>. A retried call can create a second
    tenant, so reconcile with <code>ListMyTenants</code>, which returns every
    claimed tenant the caller is an active member of, with their role. A
    membership that came from the email's domain is listed only while that
    email is verified and on a domain the tenant has verified. A self-service
    tenant cannot be renamed or deleted yet, and a member cannot leave one.
  </p>
  <p>
    A tenant admin registers and verifies email domains with
    <code>CreateDomain</code> and <code>VerifyDomain</code>. A tenant may make
    <code>GATEWAY_TENANT_DOMAIN_ATTEMPTS_MAX_PER_TENANT</code> of them
    together (default 100) in
    <code>GATEWAY_TENANT_DOMAIN_ATTEMPTS_WINDOW_SECONDS</code> (default one
    day), and both share a per-IP budget
    (<code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_DOMAIN_PER_IP</code>, default 20 per window) on
    the HTTP surface: over it a call gets HTTP 429 with a
    <code>Retry-After</code> header, which Connect clients surface as
    <code>UNAVAILABLE</code>. A host serving identity over native gRPC
    applies its own. A registered domain captures no signups until it is
    verified, and a failed re-check never un-verifies it. Registering a
    domain another tenant has verified is <code>ALREADY_EXISTS</code>, so a
    tenant admin can learn which domains are onboarded in the Project.
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing a tenant for a token</h2>
  <p>
    An access token's <code>tenant</code> claim is the deployment default
    unless the client asks for another on refresh. Selection is off by
    default; a server turns it on with
    <code>GATEWAY_TENANT_SELECTION_ENABLED</code>, on the Postgres driver
    only:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`RefreshToken(refresh_token=..., tenant_id="tenant_01HZ...")
  -> access token with "tenant": "tenant_01HZ..."  # an active member of a claimed tenant
  -> PERMISSION_DENIED, detail NOT_A_MEMBER        # not a member, or not claimed; token unspent
  -> UNAVAILABLE, detail UNAVAILABLE               # membership unreadable; token unspent
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION, detail NOT_ENABLED       # selection is off on this server; token unspent`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    A member only through their email's domain must have verified that email
    and still be on a domain the tenant has verified; moving the email off the
    domain ends the membership, and moving back does not rejoin. Every refused
    selection carries a <code>TenantSelectionRefusedDetails</code> error
    detail and consumes nothing, so a client that finds the detail refreshes
    again without <code>tenant_id</code>. <code>RefreshToken</code> refuses an
    account over its own state with the same codes and no such detail, and
    that refusal may have spent the token, so retry only when the detail is
    present. Each refresh chooses again; one without
    <code>tenant_id</code> returns to the default, and the client reads the
    tenant it got from the token's <code>tenant</code> claim. Identity's HTTP
    surface checks the tenant's status through a short cache, so suspending a
    tenant refuses its tokens there within
    <code>GATEWAY_TENANT_STATUS_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS</code> (default 30); ending
    a membership takes effect at the next
    refresh, within the access-token lifetime. A service that verifies tokens
    itself, as a host serving identity over native gRPC does, must accept a
    non-default <code>tenant</code> before its clients select one, and sees a
    suspension only when the token expires unless it checks the tenant too.
  </p>
  <p>
    The claim asserts membership only. <code>role</code> stays the account's
    role and is never a role in the chosen tenant (read that from
    <code>ListMyTenants</code>), and choosing a tenant does not apply its login
    policy, which applies at sign-in through a verified domain.
  </p>

  <h2>Invitations</h2>
  <p>
    A tenant owner or admin invites by email with
    <code>CreateTenantInvitation</code>. Every invitation sends an email, so
    each is bounded: per client IP
    (<code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_INVITATION_PER_IP</code>, default 20 per window),
    per tenant (<code>GATEWAY_TENANT_INVITATION_MAX_PER_TENANT</code>, default
    100, in <code>GATEWAY_TENANT_INVITATION_WINDOW_SECONDS</code>, default one
    day), per inviter across their tenants
    (<code>GATEWAY_TENANT_INVITATION_MAX_PER_INVITER</code>, default 100), per
    recipient across the Project's tenants
    (<code>GATEWAY_TENANT_INVITATION_MAX_PER_RECIPIENT</code>, default 10), and
    per tenant and recipient by a resend cooldown
    (<code>GATEWAY_TENANT_INVITATION_RESEND_COOLDOWN_SECONDS</code>, default
    300). Past any of these but the per-IP one the call is
    <code>RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED</code>; past the per-IP one, which applies on the
    HTTP surface only, it gets HTTP 429 with a <code>Retry-After</code>
    header, which Connect clients surface as <code>UNAVAILABLE</code>. The email names an operator's or a domain
    tenant, but never a self-service tenant, whose name its creator typed.
    Accepting an invitation never lowers a role, so it cannot demote an owner.
  </p>

  <p>
    See <a href="/identity/docs/concepts/auth-model">Auth model</a> for how the Project
    and Tenant appear in JWT claims, and
    <a href="/identity/docs/installation/configuration">Configuration</a> for the full
    set of <code>GATEWAY_*</code> variables.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
